<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- CONVENTIONS.md "Project reference graph": Contracts references nothing — never Domain,
       never BuildingBlocks. This is Reservation's entire public wire surface: the commands it
       accepts and the events it publishes (ARCHITECTURE.md "Contracts: each service owns and
       publishes its own").

       IT IS EMPTY TODAY, AND THAT IS THE ACCURATE STATEMENT OF THIS SERVICE'S SURFACE. The
       Reservation service is a Phase 0 skeleton: Reservations.Host wires Mongo, Rebus and health
       checks and nothing else, so there is no use case, no aggregate, and therefore no command
       anyone may send it and no event anyone may subscribe to. GL-25 creates the project rather
       than leaving Phase 4 to invent both the types and the packaging plumbing at once, and
       leaves it empty rather than guessing at shapes: ARCHITECTURE.md "What 'breaking' means for
       a message contract" makes a renamed type or a changed field a MAJOR version, so a guessed
       contract is the expensive kind of wrong, while adding the first real type here later is a
       purely additive minor.

       What Phase 4 puts here, from ARCHITECTURE.md "Event catalogue" and the Phase 4 plan:
       ReserveGift (request/reply) and ReleaseReservation as commands, GiftReservedV1 and
       ReservationReleasedV1 as events. Whoever writes them: ARCHITECTURE.md "Reservation
       privacy" is a product guarantee enforced by the ABSENCE of fields — no name, no email, no
       session id, no IP, nothing that could correlate two reservations to one person. A field
       added here cannot be removed again without a major bump, so the guarantee is cheapest to
       keep by never adding one. -->

  <!-- GL-25: this project is the package. Packed to the local folder feed and consumed by every
       other repo that sends Reservation a command or subscribes to its events. PackageId is the
       assembly name character for character, deliberately: ARCHITECTURE.md "What 'breaking'
       means for a message contract" makes the type name and namespace part of the wire format,
       because Rebus routes and deserializes on the .NET type name.

       Version 0.1.0 is the split's starting point; GL-27 owns the bump discipline from here. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Reservations.Contracts</PackageId>
    <Version>0.1.0</Version>
    <Authors>goodsell-engineering</Authors>
    <Description>Reservation's entire public wire surface: the commands it accepts and the events it publishes. Empty until Phase 4 — the service has no use cases yet.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
